  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mobilising Citizens to Advocate for Natural Resource and Fiscal Justice in Ghana</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will enhance citizens and Civil Society Organisations (CSOs) oversight and leadership on inclusive petroleum revenue and environmental management in the quest to manage inequalities within the sector. It will draw from our experiences on similar projects implemented under the Oil for development programme which gained some successes.  First, a system developed to monitor the performance of some selected oil companies, particularly in their commitment to gender and local content issues.  Further, the monitoring system designed, dubbed &#x2018;&apos;Nortrack&apos;&apos; would be used as a basis to stimulate public interest and discussions in the media, radio and television. This is aimed at engaging the public on the results of the tracking and get them involved in discussions to influence companies&apos; policy and practice. To bring communities&apos; perspective to our value for money audits, audio -visual evidence would be produced from communities housing oil funded projects on their views and concerns as beneficiaries of oil -funded projects, collated and used to engage with policy makers for practice change. This aims to enhance the involvement of community people, especially women, youth and other marginalised groups by project executors at project design stage to meet the needs of communities as beneficiaries. Again, from our efforts on social accountability at the subnational level, we would draw from successes and lessons from our &#x2018;&apos;Shama Model&apos;&apos; to design a tool with which the Government would be engaged for possible adoption as an accountability system at the local government (District) level.  Additionally, in our efforts to influence policy in the extractive sector, the project seeks to leverage on existing powers bestowed on traditional authorities in Ghana. By this, traditional women leaders from oil extraction areas shall engage the state on discussions on application of resources from petroleum and the ways the impacts in host communities can be mitigated, especially on c</narrative>
  </description>
